1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,625
July 13th, 1953. A mountain front

2
00:00:03,645 --> 00:00:06,231
south of Kumsong, central Korea.

3
00:00:06,347 --> 00:00:08,797
As darkness fell, the whole ridgeline

4
00:00:08,817 --> 00:00:11,090
to the north erupted in fire.

5
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:13,167
Before the guns even slackened,

6
00:00:13,187 --> 00:00:16,274
Chinese troops poured down every valley.

7
00:00:16,295 --> 00:00:18,631
By midnight, some had pushed a thousand

8
00:00:18,651 --> 00:00:21,464
meters inside South Korean lines.

9
00:00:22,029 --> 00:00:23,458
The armistice would be signed

10
00:00:23,478 --> 00:00:25,301
in just fourteen days.

11
00:00:25,355 --> 00:00:27,658
And with two weeks left, the last great

12
00:00:27,678 --> 00:00:30,236
offensive of the Korean War had begun.

13
00:00:30,618 --> 00:00:32,358
The Battle of Kumsong.

14
00:00:32,439 --> 00:00:34,729
The final battle of the war, fought over

15
00:00:34,749 --> 00:00:37,664
the last stretch of today's armistice line.

16
00:00:38,042 --> 00:00:40,496
By June 1953, the truce talks

17
00:00:40,516 --> 00:00:42,898
at Panmunjom were nearly done.

18
00:00:42,984 --> 00:00:45,343
After two years of deadlock over prisoners,

19
00:00:45,363 --> 00:00:47,789
both sides finally agreed.

20
00:00:47,889 --> 00:00:50,636
On June 8th, the prisoner exchange deal

21
00:00:50,656 --> 00:00:54,127
was signed. Peace seemed days away.

22
00:00:54,512 --> 00:00:56,524
But President Syngman Rhee bitterly

23
00:00:56,544 --> 00:00:59,790
opposed a truce that left Korea divided.

24
00:00:59,906 --> 00:01:02,812
On June 18th, he abruptly freed some

25
00:01:02,832 --> 00:01:05,977
twenty-seven thousand anti-communist prisoners.

26
00:01:06,381 --> 00:01:08,266
The communists were furious.

27
00:01:08,374 --> 00:01:10,539
Before signing, they resolved to teach

28
00:01:10,559 --> 00:01:13,122
the South Korean army a hard lesson.

29
00:01:13,291 --> 00:01:15,821
General Mark Clark believed a main aim

30
00:01:15,841 --> 00:01:19,114
was to give the ROK Army a bloody nose.

31
00:01:19,523 --> 00:01:21,708
They chose a bulge in the central front,

32
00:01:21,728 --> 00:01:24,712
pushing north toward the town of Kumsong.

33
00:01:24,842 --> 00:01:27,284
It was called the Kumsong salient.

34
00:01:27,674 --> 00:01:29,982
It was held by the ROK Capital Division

35
00:01:30,002 --> 00:01:32,531
and the divisions of ROK II Corps.

36
00:01:32,698 --> 00:01:34,442
Open to the enemy on three sides,

37
00:01:34,462 --> 00:01:36,272
it favored the attacker.

38
00:01:36,667 --> 00:01:38,150
ROK II Corps was led by

39
00:01:38,170 --> 00:01:40,550
Lieutenant General Chung Il-kwon.

40
00:01:40,689 --> 00:01:42,820
Already pushed back once in June,

41
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:45,650
his corps now faced a far bigger storm.

42
00:01:46,048 --> 00:01:49,389
To the north, five Chinese armies, some two

43
00:01:49,409 --> 00:01:52,496
hundred forty thousand men, were massing.

44
00:01:52,606 --> 00:01:53,895
Behind them stood about

45
00:01:53,915 --> 00:01:56,238
thirteen hundred sixty guns.

46
00:01:56,377 --> 00:01:57,712
After the prisoner release,

47
00:01:57,732 --> 00:02:00,456
Mao reportedly ordered a heavier blow.

48
00:02:00,846 --> 00:02:02,499
This time, the enemy had

49
00:02:02,519 --> 00:02:04,775
more men and more guns.

50
00:02:04,873 --> 00:02:06,640
It was a final gamble to draw the

51
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:09,294
line even one meter farther south.

52
00:02:09,689 --> 00:02:12,129
On July 13th, as the sun went down,

53
00:02:12,149 --> 00:02:15,361
the communist guns opened fire all at once.

54
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:17,183
In moments, the South Korean

55
00:02:17,203 --> 00:02:19,982
positions were engulfed in flames.

56
00:02:20,391 --> 00:02:22,904
From July 11th, in ten days,

57
00:02:22,924 --> 00:02:25,249
the communists fired about one hundred

58
00:02:25,269 --> 00:02:27,274
ninety-seven thousand shells.

59
00:02:27,364 --> 00:02:28,732
It was the heaviest ten-day

60
00:02:28,752 --> 00:02:30,512
bombardment of the war.

61
00:02:30,887 --> 00:02:32,901
Then the Chinese drove like wedges

62
00:02:32,921 --> 00:02:34,926
into both shoulders of the salient.

63
00:02:35,095 --> 00:02:37,283
The Capital Division in the west and the

64
00:02:37,303 --> 00:02:40,687
ROK 3rd Division in the east broke in turn.

65
00:02:41,089 --> 00:02:43,010
In the dark, the enemy slipped behind

66
00:02:43,030 --> 00:02:45,513
the lines and struck command posts.

67
00:02:45,682 --> 00:02:47,586
Colonel Yuk Geun-su of the Capital

68
00:02:47,606 --> 00:02:49,452
Division was killed trying to

69
00:02:49,472 --> 00:02:51,936
rescue his surrounded 1st Regiment.

70
00:02:52,335 --> 00:02:55,460
In the center, four enemy battalions surrounded

71
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:58,475
a battalion of the ROK 6th Division.

72
00:02:58,614 --> 00:03:00,770
Phone lines were cut. No one

73
00:03:00,790 --> 00:03:03,604
knew which positions still held.

74
00:03:03,997 --> 00:03:06,952
On July 14th, Eighth Army commander General

75
00:03:06,972 --> 00:03:09,468
Maxwell Taylor made a hard decision.

76
00:03:09,625 --> 00:03:12,120
Give up the salient, fall back south of

77
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:15,104
the Kumsong River, and rebuild the line.

78
00:03:15,504 --> 00:03:17,366
But in the rain and confusion,

79
00:03:17,386 --> 00:03:20,029
the withdrawal began to fall apart.

80
00:03:20,165 --> 00:03:21,750
Some units retreated across the

81
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:23,423
river and far beyond the line

82
00:03:23,443 --> 00:03:25,443
they had been ordered to hold.

83
00:03:25,844 --> 00:03:27,479
The enemy's next objective lay

84
00:03:27,499 --> 00:03:30,313
south: the Hwachon Reservoir.

85
00:03:30,333 --> 00:03:32,533
Lose it, and even the power plant

86
00:03:32,553 --> 00:03:34,930
lighting Seoul would be in danger.

87
00:03:35,332 --> 00:03:37,636
Behind the broken front, the South Koreans

88
00:03:37,656 --> 00:03:40,321
built a new line along the high ridges.

89
00:03:40,402 --> 00:03:41,873
It ran from Chokkun-san,

90
00:03:41,893 --> 00:03:44,500
through Chupa Pass, to Paegam-san.

91
00:03:44,878 --> 00:03:46,720
Chokkun-san rises one thousand

92
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:49,031
seventy-three meters. Paegam-san,

93
00:03:49,051 --> 00:03:51,625
one thousand one hundred seventy-nine.

94
00:03:51,794 --> 00:03:54,221
Lose these peaks, and there were no high

95
00:03:54,241 --> 00:03:57,000
mountains left between the enemy and Hwachon.

96
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,019
General Chung brought up the reserve

97
00:03:59,039 --> 00:04:00,829
11th Division and pulled the

98
00:04:00,849 --> 00:04:03,377
battered 6th and 3rd back to regroup.

99
00:04:03,458 --> 00:04:05,817
In the west, the US 3rd Division and

100
00:04:05,837 --> 00:04:10,030
the airlifted 187th Airborne rushed in.

101
00:04:10,421 --> 00:04:12,621
Meanwhile, torrential rain forced the

102
00:04:12,641 --> 00:04:15,079
Chinese to halt and catch their breath.

103
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:16,520
It gave the South Koreans

104
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:18,367
one chance to strike back.

105
00:04:18,755 --> 00:04:21,474
On July 16th, ROK II Corps

106
00:04:21,494 --> 00:04:23,307
counterattacked with the 11th,

107
00:04:23,327 --> 00:04:25,720
8th, and 5th Divisions abreast.

108
00:04:25,818 --> 00:04:28,080
Their objective was simple: retake the

109
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:30,652
high ground south of the Kumsong River.

110
00:04:31,053 --> 00:04:33,062
Climbing slopes slick with mud,

111
00:04:33,082 --> 00:04:36,032
the soldiers took back the hills one by one.

112
00:04:36,207 --> 00:04:38,145
Night after night, the enemy struck

113
00:04:38,165 --> 00:04:40,553
back hard to keep what it had won.

114
00:04:40,926 --> 00:04:42,434
Among the fiercest fights was

115
00:04:42,454 --> 00:04:45,256
Paegam-san, lost to the Chinese.

116
00:04:45,311 --> 00:04:46,794
With the 5th Division stopped,

117
00:04:46,814 --> 00:04:49,537
the attached 7th Regiment swung in from

118
00:04:49,557 --> 00:04:51,597
the north and retook the summit.

119
00:04:51,732 --> 00:04:54,831
Around Paegam-san alone, some five hundred

120
00:04:54,851 --> 00:04:58,090
seventy South Koreans were killed or missing.

121
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:00,922
On July 19th, the South Koreans

122
00:05:00,942 --> 00:05:02,583
finally stood on the high ground

123
00:05:02,603 --> 00:05:04,666
south of the Kumsong River.

124
00:05:04,733 --> 00:05:06,397
But efforts to retake the old

125
00:05:06,417 --> 00:05:08,264
positions across the river had

126
00:05:08,284 --> 00:05:10,847
to be abandoned after the 20th.

127
00:05:11,224 --> 00:05:12,879
For the final week of the war,

128
00:05:12,899 --> 00:05:16,544
ROK II Corps held this Kumsong River line.

129
00:05:16,599 --> 00:05:19,059
And on July 27th, the armistice

130
00:05:19,079 --> 00:05:21,196
was signed at Panmunjom.

131
00:05:21,597 --> 00:05:22,743
At ten o'clock that night,

132
00:05:22,763 --> 00:05:25,064
the guns at last fell silent.

133
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:26,791
The armistice line was drawn

134
00:05:26,811 --> 00:05:28,555
along the very ground where the

135
00:05:28,575 --> 00:05:30,710
two sides stood at that moment.

136
00:05:31,103 --> 00:05:34,070
That ridgeline, retaken in blood, became

137
00:05:34,090 --> 00:05:36,684
this stretch of today's armistice line.

138
00:05:36,705 --> 00:05:39,030
A few kilometers farther south, and the

139
00:05:39,050 --> 00:05:41,617
map of Hwachon might look very different.

140
00:05:42,001 --> 00:05:44,464
The salient itself was lost, about one

141
00:05:44,484 --> 00:05:46,739
hundred seventy square kilometers.

142
00:05:46,821 --> 00:05:49,202
By South Korean counts, some fourteen

143
00:05:49,222 --> 00:05:52,616
thousand ROK soldiers were killed, wounded,

144
00:05:52,636 --> 00:05:55,439
or missing. Eighth Army estimated over

145
00:05:55,459 --> 00:05:58,557
twenty-eight thousand Chinese casualties.

146
00:05:58,944 --> 00:06:01,109
So why could two hundred forty thousand

147
00:06:01,129 --> 00:06:03,578
troops never break through to Hwachon?

148
00:06:03,628 --> 00:06:05,371
Let's look at four reasons.

149
00:06:05,770 --> 00:06:07,869
First, the salient trap.

150
00:06:08,251 --> 00:06:10,273
Touching the enemy on three sides,

151
00:06:10,293 --> 00:06:11,981
the salient's shoulders were

152
00:06:12,001 --> 00:06:14,040
its weak points from the start.

153
00:06:14,170 --> 00:06:15,891
Once a shoulder broke, every unit

154
00:06:15,911 --> 00:06:18,982
inside was at risk of being surrounded.

155
00:06:19,379 --> 00:06:21,850
Second, the mountain defense line.

156
00:06:22,251 --> 00:06:24,228
The South Koreans redrew their line

157
00:06:24,248 --> 00:06:26,823
on peaks over a thousand meters high.

158
00:06:26,932 --> 00:06:27,913
Holding the heights,

159
00:06:27,933 --> 00:06:30,428
they could stand with far fewer men.

160
00:06:30,834 --> 00:06:33,962
Third, reserves and reinforcements.

161
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:35,772
The corps pulled out its shattered

162
00:06:35,792 --> 00:06:36,808
divisions and threw the

163
00:06:36,828 --> 00:06:39,380
reserve 11th Division straight in.

164
00:06:39,503 --> 00:06:41,452
With American reinforcements arriving

165
00:06:41,472 --> 00:06:44,923
within a day or two, the gap never widened.

166
00:06:45,328 --> 00:06:48,041
Fourth, the counterattack in the rain.

167
00:06:48,439 --> 00:06:50,249
The South Koreans did not waste the

168
00:06:50,269 --> 00:06:52,765
days when the enemy paused for breath.

169
00:06:52,933 --> 00:06:54,541
When the side being pushed back

170
00:06:54,561 --> 00:06:56,476
struck first, the enemy's final

171
00:06:56,496 --> 00:06:58,604
breakthrough lost its momentum.

172
00:06:58,683 --> 00:07:00,302
After July 20th, the Chinese

173
00:07:00,322 --> 00:07:02,238
advanced no farther south.

174
00:07:02,631 --> 00:07:04,780
A decade after the war, a young

175
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:07,784
platoon leader served near Paegam-san.

176
00:07:07,921 --> 00:07:09,882
In those hills, he found nameless

177
00:07:09,902 --> 00:07:12,670
graves marked only by wooden stakes.

178
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:15,741
That officer, Han Myung-hee, wrote the

179
00:07:15,761 --> 00:07:19,439
Korean art song Bimok, The Wooden Marker.

180
00:07:19,496 --> 00:07:21,069
The deep valley in that song

181
00:07:21,089 --> 00:07:22,994
was these very mountains.

182
00:07:23,544 --> 00:07:26,706
In autumn 2025, remains were recovered

183
00:07:26,726 --> 00:07:29,448
from a nameless hill in Cheorwon.

184
00:07:29,572 --> 00:07:31,168
They were Sergeant Kim Sun-sik of

185
00:07:31,188 --> 00:07:33,433
the ROK 6th Division, killed at

186
00:07:33,453 --> 00:07:36,173
Kumsong eight days before the truce.

187
00:07:36,558 --> 00:07:38,842
This May, after seventy-three years,

188
00:07:38,862 --> 00:07:41,345
he finally came home to his family.

189
00:07:41,414 --> 00:07:43,522
The line south of the Kumsong River is

190
00:07:43,542 --> 00:07:46,651
still drawn over the names of men like him.

191
00:07:47,207 --> 00:07:49,943
Fourteen days before the truce, a final

192
00:07:49,963 --> 00:07:52,947
offensive by two hundred forty thousand men.

193
00:07:53,067 --> 00:07:54,641
The line that refused to break

194
00:07:54,661 --> 00:07:57,182
before it is today's armistice line.

195
00:07:57,582 --> 00:07:59,150
If this story meant something to you,

196
00:07:59,170 --> 00:08:01,540
please subscribe and like this video.

197
00:08:01,565 --> 00:08:03,274
We'll continue the record of a

198
00:08:03,294 --> 00:08:05,460
forgotten war in the next episode.
